# Work behind a fenced index

For a project whose packages come from, and go to, your organisation's Artifactory instead of
PyPI. Nothing in the project changes for it: the fence is a set of variables your organisation
sets once{% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}, at group level,{% else %} on the repository,{% endif %} and every project under it inherits. The same
pipeline file, Dockerfile and tasks serve a project in the open with every variable unset.

## The variables

| Variable | Set where | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `UV_DEFAULT_INDEX` | {% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}the group{% else %}a repository variable{% endif %}, and your shell | uv resolves and installs from this index instead of PyPI, including the lock generation wrote. The URL is the index's simple API. |
| `UV_PUBLISH_URL` | {% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}the group, scoped to the `pypi` environment{% else %}a repository secret{% endif %} | `release.publish` uploads here, with credentials and without attestations, as [Publish to a private index](publish-to-a-private-index.md) describes. |
| `JFROG_URL`, `JFROG_PROJECT`, `JFROG_AUDIENCE` | {% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}the group{% else %}repository variables{% endif %} | Each job mints an ID token for the audience and exchanges it at `JFROG_URL/access/api/v1/oidc/token` for a token scoped to the project, as user `<project>-ci`. `JFROG_PROVIDER` names the identity provider Artifactory verifies against; `{{ git_hosting_service }}` unless set. No stored secret anywhere. |
| `PIP_INDEX_URL` | {% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}the group{% else %}not needed{% endif %} | {% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}The plan job installs uv with pip before the launcher exists; pip reads this, not `UV_DEFAULT_INDEX`. Same value.{% else %}Nothing on GitHub installs with pip.{% endif %} |
| `UV_PYTHON_INSTALL_MIRROR` | {% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}the group{% else %}a repository variable{% endif %}, and your shell | Where uv fetches the interpreters the tox matrix needs, which it otherwise downloads from GitHub. A generic remote in Artifactory in front of `github.com/astral-sh/python-build-standalone/releases/download` serves them. |
{%- if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}
| `INDEX_USERNAME`, `INDEX_PASSWORD` | repository secrets | A token the index accepts, for a project that would rather not map an OIDC identity. Set, they win over the exchange everywhere. |
| `DEPS_IMAGE_REGISTRY` | a repository variable, only for runners inside the fence | Where the deps image goes instead of `ghcr.io`: `host/repository` of a docker repository in Artifactory. The build pushes there with the index identity, and the jobs that run in the image pull it anonymously. |
| `UV_MANIFEST_FILE` | a repository variable, only for runners inside the fence | The URL of a uv version manifest your organisation hosts, in the `astral-sh/versions` format with download URLs on its generic remote; the build job, the one job outside the image, installs uv from it. |

GitHub injects none of these on its own: every workflow names them in its `env:` from `vars`,
so a variable the workflows do not name never reaches a job. An unset one arrives as an empty
string, which uv and the tasks read as unset. Repository and organisation variables are not
secrets, so a pull request from a fork sees them too, and fails on its first fetch for want of
a token; a fenced project does not take pull requests from forks.
{%- endif %}
{%- if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}
| `PYTHON_IMAGE`, `KANIKO_IMAGE` | the group, only for a runner inside the fence | The images the two deps jobs run in before the deps image exists: `python:3-slim` and the kaniko executor, from Docker Hub and gcr.io in the pipeline file. A runner that reaches only Artifactory needs both under the names its docker remotes serve them, and `DOCKER_AUTH_CONFIG` beside them. |
| `DOCKER_AUTH_CONFIG` | the group, only for a runner inside the fence | Read by the GitLab runner, not by kaniko: it is what the runner pulls a job's own image with. A build never sees it; the base image is pulled with the index identity, below. |

`JFROG_AUDIENCE` has a placeholder in `.gitlab-ci.yml`, because GitLab refuses an `id_tokens:`
entry with an empty audience and a project in the open has none to give. Variables set in the
pipeline file rank below group variables, so the group's value wins wherever it is set; the
two image names work the same way.
{%- endif %}

## What the pipeline does with them

{% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' -%}
- **Every job** mints the ID token through the pipeline's `default:` block.
- **`plan-deps-image`** exchanges it and writes `INDEX_URL`, `INDEX_USERNAME` and
  `INDEX_PASSWORD` into the dotenv it already hands the kaniko job, which passes them as build
  arguments, and `BASE_IMAGE_REGISTRY` when the index host serves the base image. The
  artifact expires after an hour.
- **`build-deps-image`** builds with them. kaniko reads one credential file, which the job
  writes with the project registry's entry and, when the plan job named it, the base image's
  registry with the same identity. The builder stage of `Dockerfile.deps` is the only stage
  that sees the credentials; the pushed image carries the synced environment and no trace,
  which `preflight` checks with `container.audit`.
- **`preflight`, `secure` and `publish`** run `index.login` first, which exchanges the token
  and writes a netrc for the index host, so the tox matrix, `uv build` and the audit's
  resolution authenticate for the rest of the job. `publish` then fills `UV_PUBLISH_USERNAME`
  and `UV_PUBLISH_PASSWORD` beside the `UV_PUBLISH_URL` the group set.
{%- else -%}
- **Every workflow** hands the fence variables down as environment, and every job that
  fetches may write an OIDC token.
- **`build-deps-image`** mints its ID token from the Actions endpoint, exchanges it, logs the
  engine in to the base image's registry when the index host serves it, and names the three
  `INDEX_*` values on the build command line while supplying them through the environment,
  so the echoed command shows none. The builder stage of `Dockerfile.deps` is the only stage
  that sees them; `container.audit` runs right after the push and checks that they stayed
  there. With `DEPS_IMAGE_REGISTRY` set the push goes there, logged in with the same identity.
- **The jobs that run in the image** pull it before any step runs, so no token can be minted
  for that pull. In the open they pull from `ghcr.io` with the workflow token; with
  `DEPS_IMAGE_REGISTRY` set the workflows leave both credentials empty, the runner skips the
  login, and the pull is anonymous, which the repository allows from inside the network.
- **`preflight`, `secure` and `publish`** run `index.login` first, which mints and exchanges
  the token and writes a netrc for the index host, so the tox matrix, `uv build` and the
  audit's resolution authenticate for the rest of the job. `publish` fills
  `UV_PUBLISH_USERNAME` and `UV_PUBLISH_PASSWORD` beside the `UV_PUBLISH_URL` secret.
- **A pull request from a fork** gets no OIDC token and no variables, so its jobs run in the
  open and fail on the first fetch. A fenced project does not take pull requests from forks.
{%- endif %}

## The container registries

`Dockerfile.deps` builds on an image from ghcr.io, pinned by digest in `pyproject.toml`. A
docker remote in Artifactory in front of ghcr.io serves it under another name: edit
`[tool.docker-versions] base-image` to that name and keep the digest, so the deps tag still
addresses the image you build on. The build pulls it with the index identity, the same
`<project>-ci` token or the `INDEX_*` pair, whenever the base image's registry is the index
host or one of its subdomains, with or without a port, which covers the three ways Artifactory
serves a docker repository. A registry on any other host, ghcr.io itself among them, is never
sent the credentials, so a project with a fenced index and an open base image keeps working.
{% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}
The deps image is pushed to and pulled from the project's own registry with the deploy token
the pipeline file asks for, which does not change. What may change is where the runner pulls
the two images the deps jobs run in before the deps image exists. On a runner with egress to
Docker Hub and gcr.io nothing is needed. On a runner inside the fence, docker remotes for both
serve them, `PYTHON_IMAGE` and `KANIKO_IMAGE` name them at group level, and `DOCKER_AUTH_CONFIG`
beside them is what the runner pulls with. kaniko never reads that variable: the base image's
credentials come from the plan job, as above.
{%- else %}
The deps image is pushed to and pulled from `ghcr.io` with the workflow token, and `setup-uv`
downloads uv from Astral's mirror at `releases.astral.sh`. On github.com with hosted runners
both are reachable and nothing more is needed. On self-hosted runners inside the fence, two
more variables move both inside: `DEPS_IMAGE_REGISTRY` names a docker repository in
Artifactory the build pushes the image to with the index identity and the other jobs pull
from anonymously, and `UV_MANIFEST_FILE` names a uv version manifest your organisation hosts,
whose download URLs point at its generic remote. The actions themselves and the OIDC token
endpoint stay on github.com, which a self-hosted runner reaches by definition.
{%- endif %}

## On your machine

Put `UV_DEFAULT_INDEX` and `UV_PYTHON_INSTALL_MIRROR` in your shell profile, then
`uv auth login <host>` with your own Artifactory identity token, which uv stores for every
request to that host. For `./workflow.cmd container.build` set `INDEX_USERNAME` and
`INDEX_PASSWORD` the same way for the one command; the build names them, never echoes them,
and logs your engine in to the base image's registry with them when the rule above matches.
Generation runs `uv lock` with the same variables and credentials, so a project generated
behind the fence locks against the index from its first commit.

## What the fence does not cover

- **The advisory database.** `secure.audit` asks pypi.org or osv.dev about each package, and
  pip-audit has no way to ask anything else. Artifactory does not proxy it. The audit needs
  egress to one of the two, or runs where it has it.
- **The docs inventories.** `document.build` fetches the Python and typing-extensions object
  inventories named in `properdocs.yml` for cross-references. Behind a fence, drop the two
  `inventories` entries or point them at copies you host.
- **The render hook's uv lookup.** Generation asks pypi.org for the newest uv to pin and
  falls back to the committed pin, saying so, when it cannot.

## The Artifactory side

What your platform team sets up, once per organisation, for every project under the fence.
This how-to names what the project maps to; the names of the repositories are theirs.

1. **An OIDC integration** for your {% if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}GitHub{% else %}GitLab{% endif %}, named as
   `JFROG_PROVIDER` says, `{{ git_hosting_service }}` unless set, with the audience in
   `JFROG_AUDIENCE`.
2. **An identity mapping** from the claims of this project's ID token to the `<project>-ci`
   user of the project `JFROG_PROJECT` names, with a token lifetime that outlasts a job.
3. **A pypi remote** in front of PyPI, whose simple API is `UV_DEFAULT_INDEX`{% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %} and `PIP_INDEX_URL`{% endif %}, which
   `<project>-ci` reads.
4. **A docker remote** in front of ghcr.io, which `<project>-ci` reads, serving the base image
   under the name `base-image` is edited to. Served under the index host, by path, port or
   subdomain, so the rule above matches.
5. **A generic remote** in front of `github.com/astral-sh/python-build-standalone/releases/download`,
   which `<project>-ci` reads; its URL is `UV_PYTHON_INSTALL_MIRROR`.
6. **A local pypi repository** the project publishes to, whose upload URL is
   `UV_PUBLISH_URL`, which `<project>-ci` deploys to.
{%- if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}
7. **Only for a runner inside the fence**, docker remotes in front of Docker Hub and gcr.io
   for the two job images, named in `PYTHON_IMAGE` and `KANIKO_IMAGE`, and credentials for
   them in `DOCKER_AUTH_CONFIG`, read by the runner.
{%- else %}
7. **Only for runners inside the fence**, a docker repository the deps image goes to, named
   in `DEPS_IMAGE_REGISTRY`, which `<project>-ci` deploys to and anyone inside the network
   reads without a login; and a uv version manifest hosted at `UV_MANIFEST_FILE`, in the
   `astral-sh/versions` format, pointing at copies of the uv releases on the generic remote.
{%- endif %}

Nothing on this list is a secret stored in the project: the token is minted per job from the
ID token, and the one identity reads packages, interpreters and the base image.

